# Valgrind suppressions for tests/ctgrind.rs — run via `verify.sh --ctgrind`.
#
# Every entry is justified below. Anything NOT listed fails the run: a
# suppression file is a record of what has been examined and permitted, not a
# silencer.
#
# The frames were generated with `--gen-suppressions=all` against the *release*
# binary that actually runs. (Generating them from a debug build does not
# transfer: different inlining gives different frames, and the file then fails
# to suppress anything while still parsing cleanly.)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Group 1 — glibc and libtest startup, reached before any test body runs
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both entries below are reached from `ctgrind::main` while the test harness
# parses its own command line and asks the kernel for its stack size. That
# happens before one line of this crate executes, so none of it can be caused by
# the poisoning these tests perform.
#
# It appears at all because the binary is statically linked against glibc, which
# is deliberate: a static binary has no dynamic linker, which sidesteps the
# `strcmp` redirection valgrind cannot set up here without root-installed libc
# debuginfo. The cost is that memcheck also sees glibc's own startup.
#
# These two are anchored on the *outer* frames (`pthread_getattr_np`,
# `test::cli::parse_opts`) with `...` in between, rather than on the full chains
# that `--gen-suppressions=all` prints. That is the fix for a real failure mode:
# the middle frames are glibc and valgrind internals (`free` and its inlined
# allocator, `__strlen_avx2`, `_int_realloc`, and on glibc 2.43 `__memchr_avx2`
# reading a partly-written /proc/self/stat line), and they change between glibc
# and valgrind versions. Enumerating them made this file version-specific:
# entries generated on 3.24 stopped matching on 3.22 (the runner's apt valgrind),
# 3.26 and 3.27, so the check failed with "unsuppressed secret-dependent branch"
# on libtest's own startup. Verified with 3.22.0, 3.24.0, 3.26.0 and 3.27.1: the
# four tests are clean on all four, and the negative control still exits 99 on
# all four -- so nothing here suppresses a real leak. `...` matches any number
# of frames.
#
# What keeps these specific: a report from *this crate* is reached from a test
# body, whose stack has neither `parse_opts` nor `pthread_getattr_np` on it, so
# it cannot match either entry. That is exactly what the negative control
# exercises.

# Static glibc's own initialisation parses LD_LIBRARY_PATH before `main` is
# reached, with an SSE4.2 string routine that reads a few bytes past each
# string's end. Anchored on `_dl_non_dynamic_init` (no compiler-generated suffix,
# unlike the `fillin_rpath.isra.0` frame above it) -- a frame that exists in any
# static glibc startup and in no other stack.
{
   libc-static-init-parses-ld-library-path
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   fun:_dl_non_dynamic_init
}

# libtest asks for its stack size (reading /proc/self/stat), and glibc's string
# routines run a few bytes past the end of the partially-filled buffer.
{
   libtest-startup-stack-size-query
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   fun:pthread_getattr_np
}

# libtest's getopts-based command-line parsing: building the option table, and
# dropping it again at the end of parsing.
{
   libtest-startup-getopts
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   fun:_RNvNtCs*_4test3cli10parse_opts
   ...
}

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Group 2 — the SIV accept/reject decision, in this crate
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These two ARE in this crate and are permitted deliberately.
#
# `decrypt` and `decrypt_in_place_detached` end in
#     if bool::from(auth_ok) { Ok(..) } else { Err(..) }
# where `auth_ok` comes from the tag, which comes from the secret key. That
# branch therefore depends on secret-derived data, and it is unavoidable: SIV
# needs the plaintext before it can compute the tag, so decrypt-then-verify is
# inherent to the mode (the crate documents this). The decision reveals exactly
# one bit that is already public — whether this ciphertext authenticates.
#
# This must NOT be read as "the tag comparison is unchecked". The comparison
# *feeding* the branch is verified separately and mechanically:
# `constant_time_eq_does_not_branch_on_operands` in tests/ctgrind.rs poisons both
# operands, requires valgrind to report nothing from the comparison itself, and
# only unpoisons the resulting `Choice` before branching on it. The timing screen
# in tests/security.rs additionally checks position-independence end to end.
#
# Matching on the mangled name means a *new* function in this crate that branched
# on secret data would not be suppressed and would fail the run.

{
   siv-decrypt-accept-reject-decision
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:_RNvCs*_20xchacha20_blake3_siv7decrypt
}

{
   siv-decrypt-in-place-accept-reject-decision
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:_RNvCs*_20xchacha20_blake3_siv25decrypt_in_place_detached
}